WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.202
A drone can use G N S S position to stabilize over the ground, display location, navigate, record tracks, and support automated behavior.

00:00:10.202 --> 00:00:16.656
These capabilities make operation easier, but they also create dependencies that operators must understand.

00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:22.694
Position hold compares the calculated location with the desired location and commands corrections.

00:00:22.694 --> 00:00:34.145
If the position solution becomes inaccurate, the aircraft may drift, correct in the wrong direction, or switch to a less automated mode depending on its design.

00:00:34.145 --> 00:00:43.097
Return-to-home relies on a recorded home point, current position, route assumptions, altitude strategy, navigation sensors, and an available recovery area.

00:00:43.097 --> 00:00:48.719
A valid-looking map icon does not guarantee that every assumption remains correct.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:54.757
Waypoint missions and geofencing depend on position and stored map or rule data.

00:00:54.757 --> 00:01:04.958
Navigation anomalies can disrupt a route, pause a mission, trigger failsafes, or place the displayed path at odds with what the crew sees.

00:01:04.958 --> 00:01:09.747
Flight logs, imagery metadata, and timestamps may inherit satellite-derived data.

00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:18.283
A navigation problem can therefore affect later mapping, event reconstruction, geotagging, or evidence even when the aircraft lands safely.

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:22.655
Timing dependency can be less visible than position dependency.

00:01:22.655 --> 00:01:33.690
If a system uses satellite time to align records, a bad time solution may create misleading event order even when the map still appears reasonable.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:45.974
Operators should know which functions require satellite navigation, what degraded modes look like, how the aircraft signals reduced confidence, and which manual skills and visual references remain available.

00:01:45.974 --> 00:01:49.929
Automation supports judgment; it does not replace it.
